+ * element and the next of the child's type, one that reads any earlier
+ * sibling ('~', :nth-child()) every later one; and the other way round
+ * for one that reads the next sibling (:last-child, :last-of-type) or
+ * any later one (:nth-last-child()). Any other element's style is as it
+ * was: a combinator only looks up and back, and the child's own style is
+ * new. A child put in at the end, under '+', '~', :first-child and
+ * :nth-child(), restyles nothing more.
+ *
